Diagnosis: Parkinsonism, cognitive impairment, recurrent falls, impaired mobility, polyarticular osteoarthritis, peripheral edema, status post hip replacement. 

Treatment: PCS [personal care aide] services of 24 hours per day, 7 days per week live in. 

The insurer denied coverage for PCS services of 24 hours per day, 7 days per week live in. 
This is the case of a female patient with a past medical history of evidence of parkinsonism, cognitive impairment, recurrent falls, impaired mobility, polyarticular osteoarthritis, peripheral edema, status post hip replacement, status post knee replacement, osteopenia, hypertension, age related cataracts, weight loss and urinary and fecal incontinence. Per the telephonic New York State Department of Health's Uniform Assessment System (UAS-NY), the patient was residing at Sub-Acute Rehabilitation (SAR) status post (s/p) right lower extremity (RLE) deep vein thrombosis (DVT). She subsequently fell sustaining a pelvic fracture. She was discharged from Rehab. 

Needs are: 

Total dependence for ordinary housework and managing finances; 
Maximal assistance for meal preparation, managing stairs, shopping, transportation, bathing and dressing her lower body; 
Extensive Assistance for phone use, personal hygiene, dressing her upper body, walking, locomotion- uses assistive devise (AD), toilet transfer and toilet use; 
Limited assistance for managing medications and bed mobility. 

She needs supervision with eating; 
Cognition: There is mild impairment. She has mild memory loss, forgetfulness and confusion in the evening. 
Continence: There is urinary but no bowel incontinence. She uses pull-ups. 

