The patient is a female with pelvic congestion syndrome requesting ovarian vein embolization.

Diagnosis: Gender Dysphoria
Treatment: Gender Affirming Surgery
The insurer denied: Gender Affirming Surgery

The patient is an adult male-to-female with a history of gender dysphoria. The patient desires chin revision and breast augmentation. She has been socially transitioning over the past five and a half years. She has been transitioning medically, taking hormone replacement therapy for greater than four years.
The only gender-affirming procedure has been electrolysis. She did have Facial feminization with
1) Frontal sinus reduction and setback;
2) Medial supraorbital rim reduction;
3) Vertical reduction of the lateral orbital rim as well as forehead reduction, frontal bone;
4) Bilateral browlift;
5) Fat grafting to the lips and cheeks;
6) Osseous genioplasty;
7) Bilateral mandible angle reduction;
8) Open rhinoplasty.

The patient reports that the male appearance of her chin, lips, chest and exacerbate her gender dysphoria and cause misgendering. Her surgeon recommends breast augmentation and chin genioplasty revision.

An enrollee has requested authorization and coverage for mastopexy (CPT 19371) and breast reduction (CPT 19318). 
the weight of the breasts being transferred to the shoulder strap area;
congenital breast deformity. In this case, the patients photographs confirm marked breast asymmetry in size before implant removal, with the right breast significantly larger than the left. Both of the patients breasts show ptosis, the right breast more than the left. The patient complains of neck, back and shoulder pain due to asymmetric breasts and of constant rash and skin irritation under her breast.

